RESEARCH CORPUS & SCIENTIFIC LINEAGE
==============================================================================

This project's cognitive state machine, deterministic question router, and
conversational alliance dynamics trace directly to the following foundational research:

1. Cognitive Architecture & Linguistic Modeling:
   - Bandler, R., & Grinder, J. (1975). The Structure of Magic I: A Book About
     Language and Therapy. Science and Behavior Books. (Foundational Meta-Model
     linguistic distortion taxonomy: Deletions, Distortions, Generalizations).
   - Bandler, R., & Grinder, J. (1976). The Structure of Magic II. Science and
     Behavior Books.
   - Dilts, R. (1999). Sleight of Mouth: The Magic of Conversational Belief Change.
     Meta Publications. (Semantic reframing patterns and Meta-Mirror systemic 4th
     perceptual position, 1988–1992).
   - Grinder, J., & DeLozier, J. (1987). Turtles All the Way Down: Prerequisites to
     Personal Genius. Quantum Leap. (1st, 2nd, and 3rd Perceptual Positions).
   - Cameron-Bandler, L. (1978). They Lived Happily Ever After. Meta Publications.
     (Codification of Positive Intent and outcome questioning sequences).

2. Empirical Validation, Socratic Inquiry & Clean Language:
   - Witkowski, T. (2010). Thirty-five years of research on Neuro-Linguistic
     Programming. Polish Psychological Bulletin, 41(2), 58–66. (Empirical boundary
     setting and de-branding protocol).
   - Passmore, J., & Rowson, T. (2018). Neuro-linguistic programming: a critical
     review of NLP research and the application of NLP for coaching/mentoring.
   - Sturt, J., et al. (2012). Neuro-linguistic programming: a systematic review of
     the effects on socioemotional outcomes. British Journal of General Practice.
   - Paul, R., & Elder, L. (2006). Critical Thinking: Tools for Taking Charge of
     Your Learning and Your Life (2nd ed.). Prentice Hall. (8-move Socratic Taxonomy).
   - Grove, D. J., & Panzer, C. W. (1989). Resolving Traumatic Memories: Metaphors
     and Symbols in Psychotherapy. Irvington Publishers. (Clean Language invariant:
     verbatim user word reuse without outside metaphor contamination).
   - Miller, G. A., Galanter, E., & Pribram, K. H. (1960). Plans and the Structure
     of Behavior. Henry Holt & Co. (TOTE cybernetic feedback loops).
   - Locke, E. A., & Latham, G. P. (2002). Building a Practically Useful Theory of
     Goal Setting and Task Motivation. American Psychologist, 57(9), 705–717.
     (Well-Formed Outcome predicate verification).

3. Working Alliance, Conversational Science & LLM Discourse:
   - Galland, L., Pelachaud, C., & Pecune, F. (2024). EMMI - Empathic Multimodal
     Motivational Interviews Dataset: Analyses and Annotations. arXiv:2406.16478.
     (Verbatim reflection and client-adaptive brevity).
   - Li, A., Wang, C., Lu, Y., Xu, R., Ma, L., & Lan, Z. (2026). CARE: An Explainable
     Computational Framework for Assessing Client-Perceived Therapeutic Alliance
     Using Large Language Models. arXiv:2602.20648. (Rationale-augmented brevity).
   - Wang, L., et al. (2025). Large Language Model-Powered Conversational Agent
     Delivering Problem-Solving Therapy (PST) for Family Caregivers. arXiv:2506.11376.
     (Resolving efficiency vs. thoroughness in Socratic dialogue).
   - Grice, H. P. (1975). Logic and conversation. (Maxim of Quantity).
   - Hayakawa, S. I. (1949). Language in Thought and Action. (Ladder of Abstraction).
   - Sweller, J. (1988). Cognitive load during problem solving. (Cognitive Load Theory).

